WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Vsauce!

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:06.759
Kevin here -- and we can predict where a burglary
will occur in Beverly Hills within a 1,000

00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:13.690
foot zone with 90% confidence a week before
it happens by using a complex algorithm that

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:18.570
tracks the frequency and location of similar
transgressions to determine the acceleration

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.660
and trajectory of escalating antisocial behavior.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:30.520
And over 1,500 mathematicians have publicly
boycotted doing ANY work on it.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:37.710
Predictive policing was named one of TIME
Magazine’s 50 best inventions in 2011 -- which

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:45.400
sounds really impressive until you discover
it’s just below the baguette vending machine.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.820
Can algorithmic prediction systems that sound
like they’re straight out of a cthonic hellscape

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.710
actually prevent future assaults and murders
like their numbers claim?

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:07.240
Or are we just using math to bully people
with the police and claiming victory?

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.670
Ensuring public safety would be pretty easy
if you knew that offenses against society

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.300
were going to happen before they happened.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.620
But it’s such a fantastic proposition that
its consequences and abuses have fueled dystopian

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:26.600
stories from George Orwell, Philip K. Dick,
and Isaac Asimov.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:33.270
We seem to inherently KNOW there’s a problem
with inescapable accountability based on potentially

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.380
flawed information.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:41.340
But real world systems for predictive policing
have actually been utilized by law enforcement

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.240
for over 100 years.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:49.030
In the early 20th century, sociologist Ernest
Burgess proposed the concentric zone model

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:55.010
for identifying and explaining sociological
patterns in urban areas, including crime.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:02:00.870
His model noted where tension and transitions
were likely to occur, like deteriorated housing

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:05.740
in the areas between a city’s central business
district and the workers’ neighborhoods

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.130
further out.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:13.170
It culminated in social disorganization theory,
which posits that people’s actions result

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.980
from their social relationships and their
environments more than their own clear, rational

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:20.120
decisions.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.980
Building upon generations of evolving sociological
theories we’ve added algorithms crunching

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.120
complex data to stop crime before it occurs.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:37.450
And the results have been…

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:42.680
The Electronic Frontier Foundation defines
predictive policing as "the use of mathematical

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:48.209
analytics by law enforcement to identify and
deter potential criminal activity."

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:54.650
We’ve got lots of data and increasingly
sophisticated analytical tools, so why not

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.010
put ‘em to use?

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.180
Here’s how we put ‘em to use.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:04.011
One group of researchers broke down the city
of Chicago into small 2-block areas, then

00:03:04.011 --> 00:03:08.510
gathered historical data on violent crime
and property crime.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:14.239
By tracking changes in those patterns -- essentially
measuring the acceleration of crime types

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:19.599
and their frequencies -- they were able to
predict where crime would occur, and they

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:23.400
replicated their results in 7 more cities.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:30.129
They describe it as tracking “spatiotemporal
point processes unfolding in social context.”

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:31.690
Okay.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:39.439
Chicago does have a well-publicized crime
problem, so this sounds like GREAT news…

00:03:39.439 --> 00:03:48.150
… until you realize the Chicago PD’s “Strategic
Suspect List” of 398,684 names of people

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:56.069
highly-likely to commit gang-related crime
was composed of only 16% gang members.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:03.910
How good are your gang-crime predictions gonna
be when 84% of your suspects ARE NOT IN GANGS?

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.019
What good is the algorithm if the data make
no sense?

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:10.560
What even ARE these tools?

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.599
Well.

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:14.020
One of them… kinda works.

