00:05:20.659 --> 00:05:25.300
Precobs is an abbreviation for “Pre-Crime
Observation System,” and it was developed

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.460
by the Institute for pattern-based Prediction
Technique in Germany.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:34.059
It identifies crimes that occur as ‘repeats’
-- like burglary.

00:05:34.059 --> 00:05:40.319
When a burglary is committed, the next crime
that perpetrator commits is… also probably

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:43.360
going to be a burglary or robbery.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.809
If there’s a car theft, there’s likely
to be another car theft -- because someone

00:05:47.809 --> 00:05:53.620
out there is good at stealing cars and they’re
unlikely to switch to robbing banks.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:59.009
Precobs feeds the data about crimes and minor
events that may have been red flags to human

00:05:59.009 --> 00:06:05.180
police officers who can then make better decisions
about where and when to patrol.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:10.779
Using Precobs, Zurich police claimed to reduce
burglary by 30% in one year.

00:06:10.779 --> 00:06:11.839
And then there’s PredPol.

00:06:11.839 --> 00:06:18.069
PredPol got its inspiration from the algorithms
used to predict earthquake aftershocks, and

00:06:18.069 --> 00:06:23.360
they’ve made grand promises about what it
can actually do: “PredPol was founded on

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.719
the audacious premise that we could help make
the practice of policing better in America.

00:06:28.719 --> 00:06:35.460
By “better” we mean providing less bias,
more transparency, and more accountability.”

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:42.020
We have heard eerily similar messaging in
the previous two videos and I’m glad I made

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:46.509
this black box for the last one, because…
we’re gonna need it.

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:51.690
PredPol, which is now called Geolitica, brags
that it’s used to “help protect roughly

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:55.449
one out of every 30 people in the United States.”

00:06:55.449 --> 00:07:00.530
Cory Doctorow compiled a list of some of the
cities using PredPol -- and dozens of other

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.139
cities have used it in secret.

00:07:03.139 --> 00:07:10.490
PredPol claims it can flag a 500ft x 500ft
area and visualize its crime history to generate

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:15.599
information for ‘quick deployment’ of
law enforcement resources -- and they even

00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:20.909
publish the algorithm right on their website!

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:24.179
Uhhh?

00:07:24.179 --> 00:07:30.770
They do mention the algorithm's 5 components,
but no one knows how it works -- or more importantly,

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:31.979
IF it works.

00:07:31.979 --> 00:07:37.259
The LAPD had nearly a DECADE to figure that
out and couldn’t.

00:07:37.259 --> 00:07:42.860
In 2019, the Los Angeles Police Department
ended its use of PredPol for what they said

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:49.449
was budget reasons -- but they also were totally
unable to evaluate its effectiveness over

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:51.419
the whole time they used it.

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:58.279
The LAPD combined PredPol with LASER, the
Los Angeles Strategic Extraction and Restoration

00:07:58.279 --> 00:08:05.509
program, to gather, analyze, and deploy as
much data as possible to identify the places

00:08:05.509 --> 00:08:08.610
and people involved in crime.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.809
So… did it help?

00:08:10.809 --> 00:08:12.449
I don’t know.

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:18.389
Look, here’s the big problem we know exists
even without being able to access the inner

00:08:18.389 --> 00:08:26.889
workings of these algorithms: You can have
very clear data and STILL get the wrong conclusion.

00:08:26.889 --> 00:08:28.879
WORLD WAR II AIRPLANES.

00:08:28.879 --> 00:08:34.620
In World War II, Abraham Wald of the Statistical
Research Group studied where to put armor

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:39.450
on planes to maximize survivability -- and
if you’ve already heard this story, the

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:46.550
math is way way more complex than you realize,
but Wald’s logical approach is important.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:51.750
The bullethole density of damaged aircrafts
were analyzed after their return home -- and

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:56.620
the areas with the most holes were the fuselage
and the wings.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:02.580
That data suggests those two areas got hit
with bullets the most and therefore needed

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.170
the reinforced armor, right?

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:06.170
WRONG.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:11.800
Wald knew that the absence of bullet holes
near the returned planes’ engines meant

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.620
the engines had probably been shot in the
planes that were shot down and never returned

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:24.000
at all -- he factored in survivorship bias,
which is concentrating on things that make

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.330
it past a selection process and ignoring those
that did not in a way that influences data-based

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:32.890
conclusions wrongly.

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:39.060
And that’s the main criticism of systems
like PredPol: a certain kind of data makes

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.630
it through the process and is reflected in
a certain kind of way.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:47.810
Then it’s used to determine action.

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:52.460
In computer science, the phrase “Garbage
In, Garbage Out” describes the problem of

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:59.100
bad or incomplete information yielding an
equally bad output -- or worse.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:01.800
It’s the same with policing.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.550
Crime statistics and related data collection
are historically…

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:07.630
not very good.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:14.060
In 2021, 4 of the largest 15 cities in the
United States reported only partial crime

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:19.690
data to the federal government -- and 5 reported
NONE AT ALL.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:20.690
ZERO.

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:27.180
In 3 of the 5 largest states, under 2% of
agencies reported their data.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.180
Was all of this just not ready?

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:30.440
Did it even exist?

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:31.740
Who knows?!

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:39.470
But bad data or the absence of critical data
completely alters our understanding of a serious

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.830
issue.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.830
The Director of the ​​ACLU Criminal Law
Reform Project summarizes the issue: “When

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:51.180
you feed a predictive tool contaminated data,
it will produce polluted predictions.”

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:57.320
It’s prone to creating destructive feedback
loops due to systematic social biases.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.550
Here’s the loop.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:10:59.550
Wanna see the loop?

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:00.550
Here’s the loop.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:05.350
If data goes into a predictive model that
over-represents a threat, then identifies

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:10.600
relevant people and places to an outsized
degree, which results in a stronger police

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:17.270
presence fueled by a preconceived notion of
the presence of crime, which results in increased

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:21.420
police interactions…what do you think happens?

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:28.200
And this is why those 1,500-plus mathematicians
and researchers just weren’t comfortable

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:31.180
working on predictive policing projects.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:37.510
There’s a growing understanding that just
like the initial read on the World War II

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:44.030
plane data, these tools might have it all
backwards -- instead of predicting crimes,

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:46.330
they’re actually predicting policing.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:52.650
Remember that amazing promise in the beginning
about predicting urban crime with 90% accuracy

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:54.550
within 1,000 feet?

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:59.150
That same paper also revealed enforcement
bias.

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:05.690
So even when the algorithm is right – we’re
left with trusting human beings to make sound,

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:09.170
fair judgments based on the results.

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:11.360
Which is its own problem.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:17.180
The Hippocratic Oath is an ancient Greek medical
text that makes physicians promise to uphold

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:22.340
ethical standards and is commonly associated
with the phrase: “Do no harm.”

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:27.760
The concept is to only prescribe beneficial
treatments.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:33.220
Should our predictive crime methods adhere
similarly to the Hippocratic Oath?

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:38.390
Do the injustices caused by them outweigh
the benefits?

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:44.150
Can we answer these questions when we can’t
even measure the benefits to begin with?

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:48.430
And how much harm do we put up with as we
figure it out?

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:54.970
Do we just have to break a few human eggs
to make the perfect algorithm omelet?

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:59.780
Do you care if it’s one big experiment on
society?

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:05.800
Does that change if it becomes one big experiment
on… you?

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.480
And as always, thanks for watching.

